WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.230
Good morning, John, I'm apparently at a water
park because it is very hot here.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:06.010
Also, because my life is amazing and marvelous
and one of the other things that's making

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.860
my life amazing and marvelous these days is
that very soon, we will have, for the first

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.010
time, non-horribly blurry pictures from Pluto.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:20.180
New Horizons has been traveling for nine years
- it was launched in 2006.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.000
In addition to containing a bunch of scientific
experiments, spectrographs, cameras, that

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.430
kind of thing, it's also carrying the ashes
of Clyde Tombaugh, the guy who discovered

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:28.430
Pluto.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.731
Which is pretty cool, he gets to like actually
go there.

00:00:30.731 --> 00:00:33.879
I think there's going to be a conversation
about this in the comments no matter what;

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:34.879
I just gotta say, Pluto's not a planet.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:39.480
And the reason that Pluto is not a planet
is the same reason why I am so excited about

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.550
the New Horizons mission: which is that Pluto
is freakin' weird.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.550
Woah!

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:52.190
The Kuiper belt is a ring of really loosely-packed,
icy, rocky worlds out beyond the orbit of

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:56.019
Neptune, and Pluto is the largest of the Kuiper
belt objects.

00:00:56.019 --> 00:01:00.889
They're so far away we've never studied the
Kuiper belt objects, except we kind of have.

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:06.220
Triton is a moon of Neptune that is just a
little bit bigger than Pluto and composed

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.040
roughly of the same stuff.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.040
[Splash!]

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.040
Failure!

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.990
Now, we know quite a bit about Triton, because
once upon a time we actually visited it.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.210
Because Triton orbits Neptune backwards, people
think that it was probably captured from the

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.530
Kuiper belt by Neptune as part of this weird
Solar-System-changing event that happened

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.170
like 4 billion years ago, in which Neptune
and Uranus switched places, also potentially

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.140
causing the destruction of like thousands
of Pluto-sized bodies.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.312
Pluto has 5 moons, uh, they're some of the
weirdest moons in the Solar System and we

00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:42.250
didn't even know it had 5 moons until pretty
recently.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.850
Before New Horizons launched we didn't even
know about 2 of them, so now it's headed on

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:51.250
it's way to Pluto and there are new moons
to watch out for, and there may be more moons

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.860
we don't even know about, which actually presents
danger.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.020
The New Horizons could crash into one of those
moons, which would end the mission, which

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.200
would be very sad.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.080
We wouldn't even know what happened, because
we wouldn't be able to get any data about

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.080
it.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.390
Those moons were probably collected on the
Kuiper belt which Pluto spends most of its

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.390
time in.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.530
A couple of them have these weird wobbly orbits
that so if you were on the surface of them,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.120
you would never know when the sun was going
to come up.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.920
Sometimes the sun would rise in the east and
then like, set in the north.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.190
New Horizons is now more than 4.5 billion
kilometers away, and to get there it had to

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.430
exit Earth's orbit faster than any object
ever has before, spend a bunch of time tailing

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.240
Jupiter and have Jupiter like suck it forward
to speed it up even further.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.280
It's going so fast now that of course it can
never slow down, so it's just gonna blast

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.310
past Pluto and deeper into the Kuiper belt.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.840
Since transmitting data from 4.5 billion kilometers
away requires a lot of fine-tuning, New Horizons

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.410
cannot take pictures and transmit data at
the same time, so as it approaches over the

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.650
next week it's going to be taking pictures
like crazy, but it won't be able to send us

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.650
any data.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.300
This is because the transmission speeds are
so terribly slow, slower than, like, an early

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.230
90s modem, it's going to take months for us
to get all the data that we're gonna get from

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:05.330
this thing, that is going to give us a truly
rich and deep and amazing understanding of

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.319
this mostly unexplored world.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:08.610
I'm very excited.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.340
John, New Horizons will be making its closest
approach on Tuesday, so if you could put a

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.459
picture on the video that would be fantastic.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:21.530
I'm really excited for everybody to see it,
just like you're excited for everybody to

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.599
see the Paper Towns movie.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:23.599
John!

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:24.599
I will see you on Tuesday!

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:25.599
[Bubbles blub blub]

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:27.220
We will of course be covering the New Horizons
mission like crazy on SciShow, and I encourage

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.231
you to also check out our new series specifically
designed for elementary school students, Crash

00:03:31.231 --> 00:03:32.109
Course Kids.

